 The new ragdoll makes the earlier absence of force available as a comparison. Pull the gold hand and the elbow stays; pull the coral wrist and the elbow follows. On release, one body retains the pose while the other falls. This difference is produced by assigned mass, rigid parts, rotating joints and contact. It does not belong to the visible outline alone.
 
 A slump can look exhausted or comic without an emotional state in the program. That is a useful question for the viewer, not proof that every visitor reads it the same way. Conversely, the presence of gravity does not certify a natural body: the puppet's unrestricted joint angles remain a deliberate simplification. The comparison can ask which constraints make a gesture sustainable, and what another set would let a body do. Its falling state is not added to the persistent gold-body record.
+
+## A chair has legs too
+
+The same pulling rule now acts on a chair, table and lamp from the existing catalogue. Their names invite a familiar use, but the joints turn where fixed construction would usually be expected. The table keeps a flat top while losing its level; the collapsed lamp continues to illuminate the floor. These are specific differences between a recognisable form, a connection and an assigned consequence.
+
+The initial support lines matter. Recognition has been staged before the visitor removes that support by taking a part. The experiment can expose an expectation without pretending it has discovered the natural behavior of furniture. What would need to be fixed, allowed to turn or made capable of breaking for another use to become possible? The lamp's continued light is also a limit of the model: electrical damage was not implemented. Shared physics permits the comparison without making a body, a chair and a table equivalent in every respect.
